@startuml "State - TCP Connection (GoF) - Class Diagram"
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
hide empty members
title GoF's own example — Design Patterns, pp. 305-313

package "before the pattern" {
  class "problem.TCPConnection" as P {
    - state : State
    + activeOpen()
    + passiveOpen()
    + send(data)
    + acknowledge()
    + close()
  }
  note bottom of P
    <b>Every operation is a switch</b>
    <b>over the three states.</b>
  end note
}
package "after it" {
  class TCPConnection <<Context>> {
    - state : TCPState
    ~ changeState(TCPState)
  }
  abstract class TCPState <<State>> {
    + activeOpen(TCPConnection)
    + passiveOpen(TCPConnection)
    + send(TCPConnection, data)
    + acknowledge(TCPConnection)
    + close(TCPConnection)
    # changeState(TCPConnection, TCPState)
  }
  class TCPClosed <<singleton>>
  class TCPListen <<singleton>>
  class TCPEstablished <<singleton>>
  TCPConnection o--> TCPState
  TCPState <|-- TCPClosed
  TCPState <|-- TCPListen
  TCPState <|-- TCPEstablished
}
P -[hidden]right- TCPConnection
note right of TCPState
  Every request is ignored by default.
  Each state overrides what it answers,
  and calls changeState() to move on.
end note
@enduml
